      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and the planet’s climate. Here’s a concise breakdown of what makes water special:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical composition** | Two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular shape** | Bent (V‑shaped) with a bond angle of ~104.5°. |\n| **Polarity** | The oxygen atom is more electronegative, pulling electron density toward itself, giving water a partial negative charge on oxygen and partial positive charges on hydrogens. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds (two as donors, two as acceptors), which gives water many of its unique properties. |\n| **Physical states** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). The transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • High specific heat capacity (absorbs heat without large temperature changes). <br>• High surface tension (creates droplets, supports small insects). <br>• Excellent solvent (especially for ionic and polar substances). <br>• Density anomaly (ice is less dense than liquid water, enabling it to float). <br>• Transparency to visible light (allows photosynthesis). |\n| **Biological role** | • Solvent for metabolic reactions. <br>• Medium for transport of nutrients and waste. <br>• Regulates temperature via sweating, respiration, and circulation. |\n| **Environmental role** | • Drives the hydrologic cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Influences weather patterns and climate. <br>• Supports ecosystems from oceans to soil. |\n| **Human uses** | • Drinking, cooking, sanitation. <br>• Industrial processes (cooling, chemical synthesis). <br>• Energy production (hydropower, steam turbines). |\n\n### Why water is so important\n\n1. **Chemical versatility** – Its polarity and hydrogen‑bonding ability allow it to dissolve a wide range of substances, making it the “universal solvent.”\n2. **Thermal stability** – The high heat capacity and latent heat of fusion/evaporation help moderate Earth’s temperature and keep organisms from overheating.\n3. **Structural support** – Ice’s lower density keeps polar ice caps afloat, protecting coastal ecosystems.\n4. **Biochemical medium** – All known biochemical reactions occur in aqueous environments; water participates directly in many reactions (e.g., hydrolysis, condensation).\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that, through its unique physical and chemical properties, sustains life, shapes the planet’s climate, and underpins countless natural and human-made processes."
